# 2. Automating the Script with a Cron Job
# You don’t want to run this script manually every time. Instead, you can automate it to run every hour. This is where cron jobs come in.

# Cron is a Linux utility that allows you to schedule tasks to run automatically at specified intervals.
# You can edit the cron jobs by typing crontab -e.
# Example Cron Job:

# To run the disk check script every hour, add the following line to your crontab:

# bash
# Copy
# Edit
# 0 * * * * /path/to/disk_check.sh
# The 0 * * * * means "run the script every hour, at the start of the hour."
# /path/to/disk_check.sh is the location of your script.
# This means that every hour, the disk_check.sh script will automatically check if disk usage is over 80%. If it is, the administrator will be notified by email.
